A Geometric Bifurcation Theory of Megafire Emergence

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Abstract

Megafires—wildfires that overwhelm all suppression resources—represent a catastrophic regime transition whose onset is poorly captured by empirical operational models. This dissertation develops a self‑contained mathematical theory that identifies the dynamical threshold separating local, controllable fires from self‑sustaining, uncontainable megafires.

A dimensionless Critical Fire Number R_f is derived from the energy balance of the governing reaction–diffusion–advection equations, expressing the ratio of combustion heat release to total cooling power. In one space dimension, a rigorous bifurcation analysis proves that travelling combustion waves appear through a saddle‑node bifurcation at a universal critical value R_f^*: below the threshold all ignitions extinguish; above it a stable propagating front—the megafire state—emerges. The parameter space is equipped with a Fisher–Rao information metric, and it is demonstrated that for a generic fold the scalar curvature diverges as the critical manifold is approached, providing a model‑based early‑warning signal that does not rely on empirical correlations. A control‑theoretic formulation using Control Lyapunov–Barrier Functions establishes that, in one dimension, a safe, stabilising suppression policy exists if and only if R_f < R_f^*, thereby giving the first mathematically precise definition of a megafire as a fire that has crossed the boundary of controllability; the analogous statement in two and three dimensions is stated as a conjecture. Finally, a Bayesian data assimilation architecture based on the Ensemble Score Filter and physics‑informed neural network emulators is specified; it estimates R_f in real time and computes the posterior probability that the landscape has entered the megafire regime.

The entire work is theoretical: every claim is explicitly labelled as a Proposition or Conjecture, all proofs are provided or sketched with references to the appropriate functional‑analytic tools, and no numerical experiments are performed. The framework is intended as a rigorous mathematical foundation upon which future predictive systems, operational early‑warning tools, and adaptive land‑management policies can be built.

Chapter 1

Mathematical Preliminaries

We collect the functional analytic, dynamical systems, geometric, and control‑theoretic tools that underpin the theory. All function spaces are real.

1.1 Function Spaces and Notation

Let \Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d (d=1,2) be a bounded domain with C^2 boundary. Standard spaces L^p(\Omega), W^{k,p}(\Omega), H^k(\Omega)=W^{k,2}(\Omega) are used. For time‑dependent functions, u\in C([0,\tau];X) or L^p(0,\tau;X). The inner product on L^2(\Omega) is \langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle. The sup‑norm on C(\overline{\Omega}) is \|\cdot\|_\infty. For brevity, \|u\| denotes the L^2 norm.

Definition 1.1 (Classical solution). A pair (T,Y) is a classical solution of a parabolic system on [0,\tau] if T,Y\in C^{2,1}(\Omega\times(0,\tau))\cap C(\overline{\Omega}\times[0,\tau]) and the equations hold pointwise. We will always work with smooth nonlinearities to guarantee local classical solutions.

1.2 Semigroup Theory for Parabolic PDEs

Consider an abstract reaction‑diffusion system

\partial_t u = D\Delta u + f(u), \quad u(0)=u_0,

where D is a positive diagonal matrix and f is locally Lipschitz. For initial data in L^2(\Omega) (or H^1), the Laplacian generates an analytic semigroup on L^2. Using the variation‑of‑constants formula and standard bootstrap arguments, one obtains local existence and uniqueness of a mild solution that is classical for positive times; see [Amann, 1985]. We assume the reader is familiar with the fact that if f\in C^2 and the domain is smooth, the solution exists globally as long as the L^\infty norm does not blow up.

Lemma 1.1 (Maximum principle for heat equation with lower‑order terms). If T satisfies \partial_t T - \alpha\Delta T + \mathbf{w}\cdot\nabla T + cT \le 0 on \Omega\times(0,\tau) with homogeneous Neumann boundary conditions and T(0)\le 0, then T(t)\le 0 for all t. Similar statements hold for the fuel mass fraction Y which is non‑negative and bounded by its initial value.

1.3 Bifurcation Theory Basics

We recall the saddle‑node bifurcation for a scalar ODE \dot{x}=r+x^2, where r is the bifurcation parameter. For r<0 there are two equilibria (x=\pm\sqrt{-r}), one stable and one unstable; they collide at r=0 and disappear for r>0. More generally, a fold of equilibria in a dynamical system occurs when an eigenvalue of the linearisation crosses zero transversely; the normal form is one‑dimensional and quadratic. In infinite dimensions, the same structure persists on a one‑dimensional center manifold (see [Henry, 1981; Kuznetsov, 2004]).

1.4 Geometric Singular Perturbation Theory

For a fast‑slow system \dot{x}=f(x,y,\varepsilon), \dot{y}=\varepsilon g(x,y,\varepsilon), with x\in\mathbb{R}^n, y\in\mathbb{R}^m, the critical manifold is \mathcal{C}_0=\{f(x,y,0)=0\}. Fenichel theory [Fenichel, 1979] states that if \mathcal{C}_0 is normally hyperbolic, it persists as an invariant manifold \mathcal{C}_\varepsilon for small \varepsilon. Folds of \mathcal{C}_0 correspond to points where normal hyperbolicity fails; they can generate canard solutions or relaxation oscillations. In our context, the slow fuel moisture will only be mentioned as a future extension, and the core analysis is on the fast combustion subsystem.

1.5 Elements of Information Geometry

Let \Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^p be a parameter manifold. A family of probability densities p(\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta}) on a sample space \mathcal{Y} defines the Fisher information metric

g_{ij}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}\sim p}\Bigl[\partial_{\theta_i}\log p(\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta})\, \partial_{\theta_j}\log p(\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta})\Bigr].

For a Gaussian likelihood p(\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{y};\mathcal{F}(\boldsymbol{\theta}),\sigma^2 I), this reduces to g_{ij} = \frac{1}{\sigma^2}(J^T J)_{ij} where J is the Jacobian of \mathcal{F}. The Riemannian curvature of (\Theta,g) measures the sensitivity of the model. A classic result [Amari, 1985] is that the Fisher metric is the unique invariant metric under Markov embeddings.

1.6 Control Lyapunov–Barrier Functions

Consider a control‑affine system \dot{x}=f(x)+g(x)u on a state manifold \mathcal{X}, with a safe set \mathcal{C}\subset\mathcal{X}. A function B:\mathcal{C}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is a Control Lyapunov–Barrier Function (CLBF) if:

1. B(x)\to\infty as x\to\partial\mathcal{C};
2. There exist a class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha and a bounded control policy u=k(x) such that \dot{B}(x;k(x)) + \alpha(B(x))\le 0 for all x\in\mathcal{C}.

The existence of a CLBF guarantees that the sublevel sets of B are forward invariant and that the system can be stabilised to the set \{B=0\} while remaining in \mathcal{C} [Ames et al., 2017].

