Chapter 2

Physical Model and the Critical Fire Number

2.1 Governing Equations

Wildfire propagation in a homogeneous fuel bed is modelled by a two‑variable reaction‑diffusion‑advection system for temperature T(\mathbf{x},t) and fuel mass fraction Y(\mathbf{x},t):

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} &= \nabla\!\cdot\!(K(T)\nabla T) - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - h(T-T_\infty) + \frac{\Delta H_c}{c_p} A\, \Psi(T) Y,\\[4pt]
\frac{\partial Y}{\partial t} &= -A\, \Psi(T) Y .
\end{aligned}
\tag{2.1}

Here K(T)=\alpha + \varepsilon\sigma T^3 combines conduction and radiative transfer, \mathbf{w} is the wind field, h a convective cooling coefficient, T_\infty ambient temperature, \Delta H_c the combustion enthalpy, c_p the specific heat, and A the pre‑exponential factor. The ignition function is taken as a smooth regularisation of the Arrhenius law:

\Psi_\delta(T) = \frac{1}{2}\Bigl(1+\tanh\!\bigl(\tfrac{T-\bar{T}}{\delta}\bigr)\Bigr) \exp(-T_{ac}/T),
\tag{2.2}

with 0<\delta\ll 1. For \delta\to 0 one recovers a sharp threshold at the ignition temperature \bar{T}. The smoothness guarantees that the right‑hand side is locally Lipschitz; the large‑activation‑energy analysis will later exploit \varepsilon = T_{ac}^{-1}\ll 1. Fuel moisture dynamics are omitted from the core model; they can be appended as a slow variable (Chapter 8).

2.2 Physical Derivation of the Critical Fire Number R_f

The ability of a fire to self‑sustain is controlled by the competition between heat release and total cooling power. Consider a control volume V that encloses the flaming zone. The net heat release rate is

\dot{Q}_{\text{release}} = \int_V \Delta H_c\, A\, \Psi(T) Y\, d\mathbf{x}.

The cooling rate consists of convective loss to the environment and conductive‑radiative loss across the boundary:

\dot{Q}_{\text{cool}} = \int_V h(T-T_\infty)\,d\mathbf{x} + \int_{\partial V} \mathbf{n}\!\cdot\!(K(T)\nabla T)\,dS.

Define the Critical Fire Number as the ratio at a representative state where the fire just begins to propagate—say, when the temperature profile resembles a front with characteristic temperature T^* and gradient scale L. After nondimensionalisation (Appendix A) the ratio becomes

\boxed{ R_f := \frac{\lambda}{\mathrm{St} + \mathrm{Pe}^{-1}} },

where \lambda is the dimensionless heat release, \mathrm{St} the Stanton number for convection, and \mathrm{Pe} the Péclet number for conduction. This definition is now independent of any linearisation; it is a genuine energy‑balance ratio. In the limit of thin flame, R_f>1 implies that heat release exceeds the sum of convective and diffusive cooling at the front.

2.3 Well‑Posedness of the 1D System

For the one‑dimensional case \Omega=(0,L) with homogeneous Neumann boundary conditions, we can prove global existence of classical solutions for smooth \Psi_\delta.

Lemma 2.1 (Global well‑posedness in 1D). Let T_0,Y_0\in H^1(\Omega) with 0\le Y_0\le 1 and T_0\ge 0. Then (2.1) with \Psi_\delta has a unique global classical solution satisfying 0\le Y(t)\le 1 and T(t)\ge 0 for all t\ge 0.

Proof sketch. Local existence follows from semigroup theory. The maximum principle yields T\ge 0 and 0\le Y\le 1, preventing blow‑up. The H^1 norm remains bounded via energy estimates. Global extension is standard (see Appendix B). ∎

