Chapter 3

Bifurcation Analysis in One Space Dimension

We now restrict to \Omega=\mathbb{R} and constant wind w>0. After nondimensionalisation (Appendix A) the system becomes

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} &= \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \partial_x^2 T - w\,\partial_x T - \mathrm{St}\, T + \lambda \Psi(T) Y, \\
\frac{\partial Y}{\partial t} &= -\beta \Psi(T) Y,
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.1}

with \Psi still smooth. We seek travelling wave solutions T(x,t)=\Theta(\xi), Y(x,t)=\Upsilon(\xi), \xi=x-ct, moving with speed c>0.

3.1 Travelling Wave ODEs

Substituting the ansatz yields

\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \Theta'' + (c-w) \Theta' - \mathrm{St}\, \Theta + \lambda \Psi(\Theta) \Upsilon &= 0, \\
c \Upsilon' &= \beta \Psi(\Theta) \Upsilon .
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.2}

Boundary conditions are

\Theta(-\infty)=0,\; \Upsilon(-\infty)=1;\qquad \Theta(+\infty)=0,\; \Upsilon(+\infty)=\Upsilon_b,

with 0\le \Upsilon_b < 1. Setting U=\Theta' we obtain a first‑order system in \mathbb{R}^3:

\begin{aligned}
\Theta' &= U, \\
U' &= \mathrm{Pe}\bigl[ -(c-w)U + \mathrm{St}\,\Theta - \lambda\Psi(\Theta)\Upsilon \bigr], \\
\Upsilon' &= \frac{\beta}{c}\Psi(\Theta)\Upsilon .
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.3}

The unburnt state P_- = (0,0,1) has a one‑dimensional unstable manifold W^u(P_-) for any c>0. The burnt state is the line \mathcal{L}=\{(0,0,\Upsilon) : 0\le\Upsilon\le 1\}.

3.2 Existence of Travelling Waves via Shooting

We look for a heteroclinic orbit from P_- to \mathcal{L}. Integrating from P_- along W^u, we obtain a unique trajectory \varphi(\xi;c,\lambda) that enters the region \Theta>0. Because \Psi(\Theta) is sharply peaked near \Theta=1 (for small \delta and large T_{ac}), the reaction occurs in a thin layer. For sufficiently large \lambda, the trajectory will reach \Theta=1 and then return to \Theta=0. The matching condition at the trailing edge—where the solution switches to the linear diffusive tail—gives a scalar equation F(c,\lambda)=0 (see (3.6) of the original draft). Geometric arguments based on continuous dependence on parameters and the fact that F(c,\lambda)\to +\infty as c\to 0 and F(c,\lambda)\to -\infty as c\to\infty (for \lambda large enough) guarantee the existence of at least one solution for each \lambda above a threshold.

Proposition 3.1 (Existence of travelling waves). There exists \lambda_{\min}>0 such that for \lambda > \lambda_{\min} there exists at least one travelling wave solution. For \lambda < \lambda_{\min} no non‑trivial front exists; all ignitions decay.

Proof. The proof uses a shooting argument combined with the fact that the unstable manifold at P_- depends monotonically on \lambda. Details are standard in combustion theory (see [Berestycki et al., 1985]) and are sketched in Appendix C. ∎

3.3 The Fold Bifurcation

The key observation is that the solution curve in the (c,\lambda)‑plane is not monotone; it bends back on itself, creating a fold. This is captured by large‑activation‑energy asymptotics (\varepsilon = 1/T_{ac} \ll 1). In the flame‑sheet limit (\varepsilon\to 0) the front speed satisfies \lambda_0(c) = \beta\sqrt{1 + 4\mathrm{St}/(\mathrm{Pe}\,c^2)}; this gives a unique c for each \lambda. For small but finite \varepsilon, incomplete combustion introduces an exponentially small correction that makes \lambda(c) non‑monotone: it decreases for small c, reaches a minimum at c^*, and then increases. The transversality \partial\lambda/\partial c=0 at the minimum is verified.

Proposition 3.2 (Fold of travelling waves). Assume \Psi is given by (2.2) with \delta\ll 1 and \varepsilon\ll 1. Then there exist a critical heat‑release parameter \lambda^* and a unique wave speed c^* such that:

· For \lambda < \lambda^* no travelling wave exists.
· For \lambda = \lambda^* exactly one (critical) wave exists.
· For \lambda > \lambda^* two waves exist, with speeds c_{\text{slow}}(\lambda) < c^* < c_{\text{fast}}(\lambda).

The upper branch corresponds to a stable front; the lower branch is an unstable nucleation threshold. Translating back to the Critical Fire Number, the threshold is R_f^* = \lambda^*/(\mathrm{St}+\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}).

Proof. The asymptotic expansion is performed in Appendix D of the original draft, replacing the step function by the smooth \tanh profile; the same jump condition holds to leading order because the reaction zone is thin compared to the thermal diffusion length. The fold emerges from the competition between the monotone flame‑sheet curve and the exponentially small correction. A rigorous geometric proof can be given using the Conley index as in [Marion, 1985]; we provide a sketch in Appendix C. ∎

Corollary 3.3 (Linear instability criterion). For the smooth \Psi_\delta, the unburnt equilibrium is linearly unstable if and only if R_f > 1 in the limit \mathrm{Pe}\to\infty and \delta\to 0. Thus the linear criterion anticipates the fully nonlinear fold.

