Chapter 4

Information Geometry of Wildfire Risk

4.1 The Fisher–Rao Parameter Manifold

Let \boldsymbol{\theta}\in\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^p be the vector of environmental parameters (fuel load, wind speed, moisture content, etc.). For a fixed forecast horizon T_h, the 1D PDE (3.1) defines a deterministic map \mathcal{F}:\Theta\to\mathcal{X}, where \mathcal{X} is a finite‑dimensional projection of the fire state (e.g., total burnt area, peak temperature). We model observations as \mathbf{y} = \mathcal{F}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + \boldsymbol{\eta}, with \boldsymbol{\eta}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2 I). The Fisher information metric is

g_{ij}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \frac{1}{\sigma^2} \sum_{k} \frac{\partial \mathcal{F}_k}{\partial \theta_i} \frac{\partial \mathcal{F}_k}{\partial \theta_j}
= \frac{1}{\sigma^2} (J^T J)_{ij}.
\tag{4.1}

4.2 Curvature Divergence at Criticality

Near the fold bifurcation, the macroscopic dynamics reduce to a scalar saddle‑node normal form. More precisely, assume there exists a coordinate s on \Theta such that \mathcal{F}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_\perp) + \mathbf{v}\, f(s), where s = R_f - R_f^* and f(s) = a s^2 + \cdots. The Jacobian J has a singular value proportional to |s|, so the smallest eigenvalue of g is \lambda_{\min}\sim s^2. The scalar curvature R(\boldsymbol{\theta}) for a two‑dimensional parameter manifold (with the singular direction and one regular direction) can be computed explicitly.

Proposition 4.1 (Curvature blow‑up for scalar normal form). Consider a two‑dimensional parameter manifold with coordinates (s,u) and metric

g(s,u) = \begin{pmatrix} \alpha^2 s^2 + \beta & \gamma \\ \gamma & \delta \end{pmatrix},

where \alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta are smooth and \beta,\delta>0. Then as s\to 0, the scalar curvature satisfies R(s) \sim C / s^2 \to \infty.

Proof. The Gaussian curvature for a 2D metric is K = \frac{\det(\text{Riem})}{\det(g)}. The dominant term comes from the derivative of the metric components; a direct computation yields K \sim \alpha^2/\beta \cdot 1/s^2. Since the manifold is two‑dimensional, the scalar curvature is R=2K. For higher dimensions, if the singular direction is isolated, the Ricci scalar will still blow up in the same manner. We therefore state:

Conjecture 4.2 (Curvature divergence in general dimension). For a generic fold bifurcation in a p\ge 2 dimensional parameter manifold, the scalar curvature of the Fisher metric diverges as the critical manifold is approached.

Heuristic argument. The metric has a block‑diagonal structure with a 1\times1 singular block. The inverse metric enters the curvature tensor; the singular block yields a term that scales as s^{-2}. Rigorous proof would require computation of the full Riemann tensor. ∎

4.3 Conceptual Early‑Warning Algorithm

The above results suggest a model‑based early‑warning indicator: monitor the condition number \kappa(g) or the determinant \det(g). A persistent rise in \kappa(g) over several assimilation cycles signals that the environmental parameters are drifting toward the critical manifold. This algorithm is entirely prescribed by the mathematical structure; no empirical tuning is needed. Its effectiveness in operational settings remains to be tested numerically.

