Chapter 5

Controllability and the Megafire Threshold

5.1 Controlled PDE and Safe Set

Suppression actions are modelled as a bounded distributed cooling rate \mu(x,t)\in[0,\mu_{\max}] added to the temperature equation:

\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \partial_x^2 T - w\,\partial_x T - \mathrm{St}\, T + \lambda \Psi(T) Y - \mu(x,t) T.
\tag{5.1}

The safe set in one space dimension is

\mathcal{C} = \Bigl\{(T,Y)\in L^2(\Omega)\times L^2(\Omega) : \int_\Omega Y\,dx \ge Y_{\text{safe}},\; \sup_{x\in\Omega} T(x) \le T_{\text{safe}}\Bigr\}.

To work with a space where the sup‑norm is continuous, we take the state space as \mathcal{H} = C(\overline{\Omega})\times L^2(\Omega).

5.2 Containable Regime: CLBF Construction for 1D

Proposition 5.1 (Existence of a safe stabilising control when R_f < R_f^*). Assume R_f < R_f^* and that the uncontrolled unburnt equilibrium is asymptotically stable in L^2. Then there exists a bounded state‑feedback \mu = k_p T (with k_p sufficiently large) and a CLBF B(T,Y) such that the closed‑loop system satisfies \dot{B} + \alpha(B) \le 0 on \mathcal{C}, keeping the fire in the safe set and driving T to zero.

Proof sketch. Because the unburnt state is stable, there is a quadratic Lyapunov function V = \frac12\int (T^2 + \gamma(1-Y)^2)\,dx with \dot{V}\le -\eta V for the linearised system. Adding a barrier term -\log(Y_{\text{safe}}-\int Y\,dx) - \log(T_{\text{safe}}-\sup T), which is smooth on \mathcal{C}, yields B = V + \nu\Phi. The high‑gain linear feedback k_p T ensures that near the boundary the control dominates the nonlinearity, keeping B bounded. A complete verification using infinite‑dimensional barrier functions can be found in Appendix E. ∎

5.3 Uncontainability for R_f > R_f^*

Proposition 5.2 (Uncontainability in 1D). If R_f > R_f^*, then for any admissible control \mu(x,t)\le \mu_{\max} there exist initial conditions in \mathcal{C} such that the solution leaves \mathcal{C} in finite time. In particular, no CLBF exists.

Proof. Let (\Theta_*,\Upsilon_*) be the stable travelling wave from Proposition 3.2, with speed c>0. On a sufficiently long domain, embed a segment of this wave as initial condition, with T\le T_{\text{safe}} and total fuel above Y_{\text{safe}}. Consider the worst‑case control \mu\equiv\mu_{\max}. By the parabolic comparison principle, the solution with this maximum cooling is a supersolution of the system with reduced heat release \lambda' = \lambda - \delta chosen such that the modified wave speed is still positive. Hence the fire front propagates at least at speed c_\delta>0, consuming fuel until the safe set is violated. Since any actual control is weaker than the constant maximal cooling, the true trajectory also escapes \mathcal{C}. ∎

Conjecture 5.3 (2D/3D uncontainability). For spatial dimension d\ge 2, the same uncontainability result holds: when R_f > R_f^*, no bounded control can keep the system within a sensible safe set for all initial conditions, because stable planar travelling waves exist and propagate regardless of bounded cooling.

