Chapter 6

Bayesian Data Assimilation Architecture

We outline the mathematical structure of a filter that could estimate R_f from partial observations. No numerical implementation is attempted.

6.1 State‑Space Model

The environmental parameters \boldsymbol{\theta} evolve slowly:

d\boldsymbol{\theta} = b(\boldsymbol{\theta})dt + \Sigma\,dW_t .

The fire state over one assimilation window is treated as a deterministic function \mathbf{x}_k = \mathcal{F}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k; \mathbf{x}_{k-1}) given by the PDE model (or an emulator). Observations are \mathbf{y}_k = H\mathbf{x}_k + \boldsymbol{\eta}_k, \boldsymbol{\eta}_k\sim\mathcal{N}(0,R_k).

6.2 Ensemble Score Filter (EnSF)

The EnSF [Bao et al., 2024] represents the filtering distribution by an ensemble of particles. Given a prior ensemble \{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k-1}^{(i)}\}, the prediction step uses the stochastic dynamics. The update step transports the prior ensemble to posterior samples using a score‑based generative model, requiring the score \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p(\mathbf{y}_k|\boldsymbol{\theta}). For a Gaussian likelihood, this gradient is computed by back‑propagating through the map \mathcal{G}=H\circ\mathcal{F}.

Proposition 6.1 (Consistency of EnSF under ideal conditions). Assume the forward map \mathcal{F} is continuously differentiable, the observation noise covariance is positive definite, and the score matching is solved exactly with an infinite ensemble. Then the posterior ensemble converges in distribution to the true Bayesian posterior p(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k|\mathbf{y}_{1:k}).

Proof. This follows from the properties of score‑based diffusion models and Bayes’ rule; see [Bao et al., 2024] for a detailed proof. ∎

6.3 Physics‑Informed Neural Network Emulator

To make the filter fast, the map \mathcal{F} is replaced by a PINN emulator u_\phi trained to satisfy the PDE residual.

Proposition 6.2 (Existence of an accurate emulator). Let u=(T,Y) be the classical solution of (3.1) on a bounded domain. For any \varepsilon>0 there exists a neural network u_\phi with smooth activations such that \|u_\phi - u\|_{L^\infty([0,\tau];L^2)} < \varepsilon.

Proof sketch. The universal approximation theorem guarantees that the PINN loss can be made arbitrarily small; standard energy estimates then bound the solution error by the residual [Raissi et al., 2019]. Because we use the smooth \Psi_\delta, classical solutions exist and the argument holds. ∎

The resulting architecture cycles: (1) ingest observation \mathbf{y}_k; (2) update ensemble via EnSF; (3) compute R_f^{(i)} for each member and the posterior probability P_{\text{mega}}(t_k)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i \mathbf{1}_{\{R_f^{(i)} > R_f^*\}}; (4) monitor the Fisher metric condition number \kappa(g) from the ensemble covariance to issue early warnings.

