Appendix A

Nondimensionalisation and Energy‑Flux Derivation of the Critical Fire Number R_f

We present the complete nondimensionalisation of the dimensional system (2.1) and show that R_f emerges naturally as the ratio of the heat release rate to the total linear dissipation rate in the energy balance of a stationary front.

A.1 Dimensional Equations

The dimensional model is

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} &= \nabla\!\cdot\!(K(T)\nabla T) - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - h(T-T_\infty) + \frac{\Delta H_c}{c_p} A \, \Psi(T) Y , \\[4pt]
\frac{\partial Y}{\partial t} &= -A \, \Psi(T) Y ,
\end{aligned}

where
K(T) = \alpha + \varepsilon\sigma T^3  (thermal conductivity plus radiative transfer),
\mathbf{w} is the wind velocity,
h is the convective heat transfer coefficient,
T_\infty is the ambient temperature,
\Delta H_c is the heat of combustion per unit mass of fuel,
c_p is the specific heat of air,
A is the pre‑exponential factor, and
\Psi(T) is a smooth ignition function (2.2).

A.2 Reference Scales

Choose characteristic scales:

· Length L : typical fuel bed depth or flame width.
· Velocity U : a characteristic wind speed (or the speed of the fire itself; for advection‑dominated fires, U is the wind speed).
· Time \tau = L/U.
· Temperature rise \Delta T_c = T_c - T_\infty where T_c is a representative combustion temperature (e.g., the temperature at which the reaction rate peaks).
· Fuel mass fraction Y_0 : initial fuel mass fraction (usually 1).

Define dimensionless variables (indicated by tildes, then dropped)

\tilde{T} = \frac{T-T_\infty}{\Delta T_c}, \qquad
\tilde{Y} = \frac{Y}{Y_0}, \qquad
\tilde{\mathbf{x}} = \frac{\mathbf{x}}{L}, \qquad
\tilde{t} = \frac{t}{\tau}, \qquad
\tilde{\mathbf{w}} = \frac{\mathbf{w}}{U}.

The thermal diffusivity is scaled as \kappa(\tilde{T}) = K(T)/\alpha_0, where \alpha_0 is a reference diffusivity (e.g., \alpha/c_p\rho at ambient conditions). For simplicity we take \alpha_0 = \alpha/(\rho c_p) and incorporate radiative effects into a temperature‑dependent factor.

A.3 Dimensionless Groups

Substituting and simplifying yields the dimensionless system

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} &= \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \nabla\!\cdot\!(\kappa(T)\nabla T) - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - \mathrm{St}\, T + \lambda \Psi(T) Y , \\[4pt]
\frac{\partial Y}{\partial t} &= -\beta \Psi(T) Y ,
\end{aligned}
\tag{A.1}

where we have dropped the tildes and the dimensionless parameters are

\mathrm{Pe} = \frac{UL}{\alpha_0}, \qquad
\mathrm{St} = \frac{hL}{U}, \qquad
\lambda = \frac{A L \Delta H_c Y_0}{c_p \Delta T_c U}, \qquad
\beta = \frac{A L}{U}.

Physically:

· \mathrm{Pe} (Péclet number): ratio of advective to conductive heat transport.
· \mathrm{St} (Stanton number): ratio of convective cooling to advective enthalpy transport.
· \lambda: dimensionless heat release rate; the available combustion energy relative to the energy carried by advection.
· \beta: dimensionless fuel consumption rate.

A.4 Energy‑Balance Derivation of R_f

To motivate the Critical Fire Number, we consider a control volume V that encloses a steady propagating fire front. In the frame moving with the front, the temperature field is stationary. Integrating the steady‑state version of (A.1) over V and applying the divergence theorem gives

\int_{\partial V} \!\!\left( \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \kappa(T)\nabla T - \mathbf{w} T \right)\!\cdot\!\mathbf{n}\,dS
\;-\; \int_V \mathrm{St}\, T \,d\mathbf{x}
\;+\; \lambda \int_V \Psi(T) Y \,d\mathbf{x} = 0 .

The first term represents the net diffusive and advective flux of heat out of V. For a front propagating to the right, the dominant fluxes occur at the left (burnt) and right (unburnt) boundaries. The diffusive flux at the unburnt side is small; the advective flux at the burnt side carries heat away. Scaling arguments show that the net flux is of the same order as the convective cooling term. Collectively, the sum of diffusive and convective losses scales like (\mathrm{St} + 1/\mathrm{Pe}) \int_V T d\mathbf{x}. The heat release scales like \lambda \int_V \Psi Y. Balancing these yields a critical condition when

\lambda \sim \mathrm{St} + \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} .

We therefore define the dimensionless Critical Fire Number

\boxed{ R_f := \frac{\lambda}{\mathrm{St} + \mathrm{Pe}^{-1}} } .
\tag{A.2}

If R_f > 1, the heat release exceeds the total linear dissipation, and the front can be self‑sustaining; if R_f < 1, cooling dominates and any ignition dies out. This definition is completely independent of any linearisation; it stems from the global energy balance of the front.

Remark A.1. In the limit of negligible diffusion (\mathrm{Pe}\to\infty), R_f = \lambda/\mathrm{St}; in the limit of no convection (\mathrm{St}=0), R_f = \lambda\,\mathrm{Pe}. Thus R_f interpolates between the two pure cases.

