Appendix B

Well‑Posedness of the 1D Reaction–Diffusion–Advection System

We prove global existence and uniqueness of classical solutions for the one‑dimensional system on a bounded interval \Omega=(0,L) with homogeneous Neumann boundary conditions. The ignition function \Psi(T) is taken as the smooth regularisation (2.2), hence it is globally Lipschitz on bounded temperature intervals.

B.1 Local Existence

Consider the system

\begin{aligned}
\partial_t T &= \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \partial_x^2 T - w \partial_x T - \mathrm{St}\, T + \lambda \Psi(T) Y , \\
\partial_t Y &= -\beta \Psi(T) Y , \\
\partial_x T(0,t) &= \partial_x T(L,t)=0 , \quad T(x,0)=T_0(x),\; Y(x,0)=Y_0(x).
\end{aligned}

We treat this as an abstract semilinear parabolic equation in the space X = C(\overline{\Omega}) \times C(\overline{\Omega}). The linear part A T = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \partial_x^2 T - w \partial_x T - \mathrm{St}\, T with Neumann boundary conditions generates an analytic semigroup on C(\overline{\Omega}) (see [Amann, 1985]). The nonlinearity F(T,Y) = (\lambda \Psi(T) Y,\; -\beta \Psi(T) Y) is locally Lipschitz because \Psi is smooth and Y is bounded between 0 and 1. By standard semigroup theory (e.g., [Henry, 1981]), for any initial data in C(\overline{\Omega}) there exists a unique local mild solution that is classical for t>0. With T_0,Y_0\in H^1(\Omega), the solution has higher regularity.

B.2 A Priori Bounds

1. Positivity of Y: From \partial_t Y = -\beta \Psi(T) Y \ge -\beta C Y, Grönwall’s inequality and Y_0\ge 0 imply Y\ge 0. Moreover, \partial_t Y \le 0 because \Psi(T)\ge 0, so Y(x,t) \le Y_0(x) \le 1. Hence 0\le Y\le 1 for all times.
2. Positivity of T: The temperature equation is a linear parabolic equation with a source term \lambda\Psi(T)Y\ge 0. Since the initial temperature is non‑negative and Neumann boundary conditions apply, the weak maximum principle guarantees T(x,t)\ge 0. (A detailed proof can be given by considering the equation for T^- = \max(0,-T) and showing it vanishes.)
3. L^\infty bound on T: The reaction term \lambda \Psi(T) Y \le \lambda (since \Psi(T)\le 1 and Y\le 1). Thus T satisfies \partial_t T \le \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \partial_x^2 T - w \partial_x T - \mathrm{St}\, T + \lambda. The comparison principle for parabolic equations gives T(x,t) \le \bar{T}(t), where \bar{T} solves \dot{\bar{T}} = -\mathrm{St}\, \bar{T} + \lambda with \bar{T}(0)=\max T_0. Hence \bar{T}(t) \le \max\{\max T_0,\, \lambda/\mathrm{St}\}. So T is uniformly bounded in L^\infty.

B.3 Global Existence

Because the solution remains bounded in the supremum norm, the Lipschitz constants of the nonlinearity remain bounded, and the local solution can be extended indefinitely. A standard continuation argument yields a unique global classical solution.

Lemma B.1. For initial data T_0,Y_0\in H^1(\Omega) with 0\le Y_0\le 1 and T_0\ge 0, the 1D system possesses a unique global classical solution satisfying 0\le Y(x,t)\le 1 and T(x,t)\ge 0 for all t\ge 0. Moreover, the solution depends continuously on the initial data in the H^1-norm.

Proof. The above a priori bounds prevent blow‑up; the continuation theorem for semilinear parabolic equations (see [Amann, 1985, Theorem 10.1]) then gives global existence. Continuous dependence follows from energy estimates. ∎

Remark B.2. The same proof extends to two space dimensions provided the domain is smooth and one uses maximum principles for the Laplacian. However, in 2D we may only obtain L^p bounds and require more regularity for the advection term. Since the core bifurcation analysis is in 1D, we do not elaborate.

