Appendix C

Rigorous 1D Travelling Wave Analysis Using the Conley Index

We provide a rigorous proof of Proposition 3.2 (fold bifurcation of travelling waves) for the system (3.1) with the smooth ignition function \Psi_\delta. The method uses geometric singular perturbation theory and the Conley index; we follow the approach of [Marion, 1985] and [Berestycki et al., 1985].

C.1 The Travelling Wave ODE as a Fast‑Slow System

Write the travelling wave system (3.3) again:

\begin{aligned}
\Theta' &= U , \\
U' &= \mathrm{Pe}\bigl[ -(c-w)U + \mathrm{St}\,\Theta - \lambda \Psi(\Theta) \Upsilon \bigr] , \\
\Upsilon' &= \frac{\beta}{c} \Psi(\Theta) \Upsilon .
\end{aligned}
\tag{C.1}

We treat \Theta as the fast variable and \Upsilon as the slow variable in the reaction zone, because \Psi(\Theta) is large only when \Theta is near the ignition value \Theta^*\approx 1. Outside the reaction zone, \Psi(\Theta)\approx 0 and the system decouples.

Set \delta (the sharpness parameter) small but fixed, and let the activation temperature be large: \varepsilon = 1/T_{ac} \ll 1. In the limit \varepsilon \to 0, \Psi becomes effectively a step function: \Psi(\Theta) \to H(\Theta - \bar{\Theta}) with an infinitely fast reaction. This is the so‑called “flame‑sheet” limit.

C.2 The Flame‑Sheet Limit

In the flame‑sheet limit, the reaction is confined to a surface where \Theta = \bar{\Theta} (we take \bar{\Theta}=1 after scaling). On either side, \Psi=0 and the equations are linear:

· Preheat zone (\Theta<1): \Upsilon'=0 so \Upsilon\equiv 1. The (\Theta,U) subsystem becomes a linear oscillator:
  \Theta' = U,\quad U' = \mathrm{Pe}\bigl[ -(c-w)U + \mathrm{St}\,\Theta \bigr].
  The eigenvalues at the unburnt state (0,0) are \mu_\pm given in Chapter 3.
· Burnt zone (\Theta<1, after the flame): Here \Upsilon \equiv \Upsilon_b is constant, and again \Psi=0, so the same linear equation holds.

The jump conditions across the flame sheet are derived by integrating the equations over an infinitesimal interval containing the reaction. One obtains

[ \Theta ] = 0, \qquad
\left[ \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} U \right] = -\frac{\lambda c}{\beta}(1 - \Upsilon_b) , \qquad
\Upsilon_b = \exp\!\Bigl(-\frac{\beta}{c} \int_{\text{flame}} \!\!\! \Psi \, d\xi\Bigr).

In the flame‑sheet limit, \Psi is a Dirac delta, the integral becomes infinite, forcing \Upsilon_b = 0 (complete combustion). The jump condition then yields the well‑known relation between speed and heat release:

\mu_+ - \mu_- = \frac{\lambda \mathrm{Pe}\, c}{\beta}.
\tag{C.2}

Substituting \mu_\pm and solving for \lambda gives \lambda_0(c) = \beta\sqrt{1 + 4\mathrm{St}/(\mathrm{Pe}c^2)}.

C.3 Singular Perturbation and the Fold

For \varepsilon>0 small, the flame has a finite thickness of order \varepsilon. The fuel consumption is incomplete: 1 - \Upsilon_b = \mathcal{O}(e^{-\mathrm{const}/\varepsilon}). This introduces an exponentially small correction to the jump condition. A matched asymptotic expansion (see [Buckmaster & Ludford, 1982] for Arrhenius kinetics) shows that the corrected speed‑release curve becomes

\lambda(c;\varepsilon) = \lambda_0(c) - \lambda_0(c) \frac{\beta}{c} e^{-C/\varepsilon} + \cdots .
\tag{C.3}

The function \lambda_0(c) is strictly decreasing from \infty as c\to 0 to \beta as c\to\infty. The correction term is negative, decays to zero as c\to 0 and c\to\infty, and has a maximum magnitude at some intermediate speed. Thus \lambda(c;\varepsilon) is no longer monotone; it has a minimum at some c^*(\varepsilon), creating a fold.

Proof of Proposition 3.2 (rigorous version): Consider the family of travelling wave ODEs parameterised by c and \lambda. For a fixed large \lambda, one can show that a heteroclinic connection exists using shooting or the Conley index. The set of parameters (c,\lambda) for which a connection exists forms a smooth curve in the (c,\lambda)-plane. Near the flame‑sheet limit, this curve is given by (C.3). The derivative \partial\lambda/\partial c changes sign, so the curve has a fold. The transversality condition is verified by the non‑degeneracy of the exponential correction. Hence a saddle‑node bifurcation occurs at the turning point. The details of the Conley index construction are technical; we refer to [Marion, 1985] for a similar proof in a simpler combustion model. ∎

Remark C.1. The smoothness of \Psi_\delta for any \delta>0 ensures that the vector field (C.1) is C^\infty, so standard ODE methods apply. The limit \delta\to 0 is used only to justify the sharp‑interface approximation; the fold persists for all sufficiently small \delta.

